    {
      "text": "The test comes directly from section 43 of the Trade Marks Act 1995: an application must be rejected if, because of a connotation of the trade mark or a sign it contains, use of the mark in relation to the particular goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s43",
          "quote": "An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "The supplied Manual discussion of a ‘real tangible danger’ rather than a mere possibility appears in its section 44 discussion, where it attributes that formulation to French J in Registrar of Trade Marks v Woolworths Limited, restating propositions from Kitto J in Southern Cross Refrigerating Co v Toowoomba Foundry Pty Ltd.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/2#1",
          "quote": "A presumption of registrability applies to section 44 of the Act."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/2#1",
          "quote": "In Registrar of Trade Marks v Woolworths Limited [1999] FCA 1020, French J referred to the tests set out in Southern Cross Refrigerating Co v Toowoomba Foundry Pty Ltd [1954] HCA 82 (Southern Cross), where Kitto J set out a number of propositions often used to assess the likelihood of deception or confusion."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/2#1",
          "quote": "French J restated these as follows at [50]: (i) To show that a trade mark is deceptively similar to another it is necessary to show that a real tangible danger of deception and confusion occurring. A mere possibility is not sufficient."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
